rd.\n- **Play B1**: Same logic \u2014 B1+B2 can't form chow without B3. Also, B1 is low value otherwise.\n- **Play W2**: I have one W2. Opponent across has a chow [W2,W3,W4] exposed, meaning they already used a W2. Another W2 might be risky if someone else is collecting, but unshown W2 count is 1, so only one remains unseen. Probably safe.\n- **Play T5**: T5 is relatively scarce (unshown: 2 left). Opponents have discarded T5 multiple times (across and previous player). No indication of strong T suit collection. But T5 could help in middle sequences.\n- **Play J3**: I have one J3. J3 is White Dragon. J1 and J2 are all gone (unshown: 0). J3 unshown: 1 \u2192 only one left (mine). So J3 is rare. Discarding it removes chance of forming a Dragon Pung later. But I have no other dragons, so unlikely to complete pung unless I draw/kong later. However, discarding it now gives up potential.\n\nBut note: I already have three melds and five single tiles. I must discard one to maintain 13 tiles. I am one tile away from completing the fourth meld or pair.\n\nWait \u2014 hand shows: [W2:1, T5:1, B1:1, B2:1, J3:1] \u2192 five singles. That means I currently have 3 melds (9 tiles) + 5 singles = 14 tiles? Impossible.\n\nAh \u2014 correction: In Mahjong, players hold 13 tiles before drawing. After drawing, 14 tiles, then discard one.\n\nBut here, valid actions include \"Play X\", meaning I must discard one.\n\nSo current hand: 13 tiles? But listed as five: W2, T5, B1, B2, J3 \u2192 only 5? And 3 melds \u00d7 3 = 9 \u2192 total 14. Contradiction.\n\nUnless... the melds are exposed, so not in hand. Hand tiles are only the concealed ones.\n\nSo yes: hand = 5 tiles, plus 3 exposed melds (9 tiles) \u2192 total 14. So I must have just drawn. Now I must discard one.\n\nSo goal: evaluate which discard minimizes risk and maintains potential for high-scoring hand.\n\nBest possible pattern in reach:\n- **Half Flush**? Not likely \u2014 too many suits.\n- **All Types**? I have W (Characters), T (